    <header class="methodology-header">
      <div class="methodology-eyebrow">Methodology</div>
      <h1 class="methodology-title">How AEO structures conference content</h1>
      <p class="methodology-lede">
        Six stages that turn one Airspace World session into an asset AI engines can find,
        understand, and cite. This is the same methodology applied across every page on this hub.
      </p>
    </header>

    <ol class="methodology-stages" aria-label="The six stages of AEO content structuring">

      <li class="stage" id="stage-1">
        <div class="stage-number" aria-hidden="true">01</div>
        <div class="stage-content">
          <h2 class="stage-title">Raw content capture</h2>
          <p class="stage-summary">
            Sessions begin as recordings, slide decks, and notes — formats AI engines cannot read directly.
            The first task is comprehensive content capture: full transcripts, slide text extraction,
            and structured metadata about who spoke, when, and on what.
          </p>
          <div class="stage-example">
            <strong>Example from this hub:</strong> Each ASW 2026 session is captured with its full
            title, speakers, organisation affiliations, scheduled time, and theme alignment.
          </div>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="stage" id="stage-2">
        <div class="stage-number" aria-hidden="true">02</div>
        <div class="stage-content">
          <h2 class="stage-title">Entity extraction</h2>
          <p class="stage-summary">
            Within raw content, identify the entities AI engines recognise: people, organisations,
            technologies, regulations, geographic locations. These become the connection points
            that link your content to the broader knowledge graph AI engines maintain.
          </p>
          <div class="stage-example">
            <strong>Example from this hub:</strong> A session on U-space mentions EASA, the U-space
            Service Provider regulation (EU 2021/664), and specific ANSPs. Each becomes a tagged
            entity that AI engines can match against existing knowledge.
          </div>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="stage" id="stage-3">
        <div class="stage-number" aria-hidden="true">03</div>
        <div class="stage-content">
          <h2 class="stage-title">Question-and-answer restructuring</h2>
          <p class="stage-summary">
            AI engines disproportionately cite content formatted as questions with self-contained answers.
            Restructure session content to lead with the questions the session answered — phrased
            the way a journalist or professional would actually search for them.
          </p>
          <div class="stage-example">
            <strong>Example from this hub:</strong> A session on AI in ATC becomes five Q&A pairs
            including <em>"What is the EASA AI certification framework?"</em> and <em>"Which AI tools
            are operational in European ANSPs in 2026?"</em>
          </div>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="stage" id="stage-4">
        <div class="stage-number" aria-hidden="true">04</div>
        <div class="stage-content">
          <h2 class="stage-title">Schema markup</h2>
          <p class="stage-summary">
            Structured Q&A becomes machine-readable through JSON-LD schema markup — specifically
            FAQPage, Person, Event, and Article types. This is the layer most sites miss. Without
            it, even well-written content struggles for AI citation. With it, content becomes
            extractable as discrete, citable data points.
          </p>
          <div class="stage-example">
            <strong>Example from this hub:</strong> Every theme page carries FAQPage JSON-LD with
            five questions. Every speaker page carries Person schema with role, organisation, and
            session attribution.
          </div>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="stage" id="stage-5">
        <div class="stage-number" aria-hidden="true">05</div>
        <div class="stage-content">
          <h2 class="stage-title">Internal linking for topical authority</h2>
          <p class="stage-summary">
            AI engines reward sites that comprehensively cover a topic from multiple angles. Each
            theme links to relevant sessions. Each session links to its speakers. Each speaker links
            to their session and theme. This interconnected structure signals topical authority —
            the basis on which AI engines decide which source to cite.
          </p>
          <div class="stage-example">
            <strong>Example from this hub:</strong> The "AI in Air Traffic Control" theme connects
            to two sessions, three speakers, and five FAQ entries — creating a knowledge graph AI
            engines treat as authoritative on the topic.
          </div>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="stage" id="stage-6">
        <div class="stage-number" aria-hidden="true">06</div>
        <div class="stage-content">
          <h2 class="stage-title">Publishing for AI discoverability</h2>
          <p class="stage-summary">
            Final step: the content goes live with proper canonical URLs, sitemap registration,
            llms.txt declarations, and submission to Google Search Console and Bing Webmaster Tools.
            Bing matters specifically because it feeds Perplexity's citation engine. AI engines need
            to know the content exists before they can cite it.
          </p>
          <div class="stage-example">
            <strong>Example from this hub:</strong> Every page is registered in the sitemap, declared
            in llms.txt with topic categorisation, and indexed across both Google and Bing within
            72 hours of publication.
          </div>
        </div>
      </li>

    </ol>

